# 🔹 Queue in C (FIFO)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

// Node structure for linked list
struct Node {
    int data;              // value
    struct Node* next;     // pointer to next node
};

// Global pointers to represent queue front and rear
struct Node* front = NULL;
struct Node* rear = NULL;

// Function to enqueue (insert element at rear)
void enqueue(int value) {
    struct Node* newNode = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    if (newNode == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed!\n");
        return;
    }
    newNode->data = value;
    newNode->next = NULL;

    if (rear == NULL) { // queue is empty
        front = rear = newNode;
    } else {
        rear->next = newNode;   // attach new node at the end
        rear = newNode;         // update rear
    }
    printf("%d enqueued into queue\n", value);
}

// Function to dequeue (remove element from front)
void dequeue() {
    if (front == NULL) { // empty queue
        printf("Queue Underflow! Cannot dequeue.\n");
        return;
    }
    printf("%d dequeued from queue\n", front->data);
    struct Node* temp = front;   // store old front
    front = front->next;         // move front
    if (front == NULL)           // if queue is now empty
        rear = NULL;
    free(temp);                  // free memory
}

// Function to peek (see front element)
void peekQueue() {
    if (front == NULL) {
        printf("Queue is empty.\n");
        return;
    }
    printf("Front element is: %d\n", front->data);
}

// Function to check if queue is empty
int isQueueEmpty() {
    return front == NULL;
}
```

---

### 🔎 Step-by-Step Explanation
1. `front` → pointer to first element (to be dequeued).  
2. `rear` → pointer to last element (where new items get enqueued).  
3. `enqueue(int value)` → creates a node, attaches it at the end, updates `rear`.  
   - If queue was empty, both `front` and `rear` point to new node.  
4. `dequeue()` → removes front node, updates `front`, frees memory.  
   - If queue becomes empty, set `rear = NULL`.  
5. `peekQueue()` → prints the value at `front`.  
6. `isQueueEmpty()` → returns `1` if queue is empty.  

